.chapter 5
.h1 Memory and speed [memory]
Two rounds of work cut the memory and the time of the Editor.  The first, on
11 and 12 November, removed two large mistakes.  The second, on 18 November,
removed a third of the memory.  The numbers are those of the notes of the
measurements.
.h2 Large-document fixes [memory-first]
The first test was a document of 15.6 MB, the complete works of Mark Twain
from Project Gutenberg.
Open time: on 11 November the Editor took @early_open_before@ s to open it.
The function that gives the level of a block scanned the document from its
start for every block, so the layout took time with the square of the number
of blocks, about 48,000.  The level is now read from a table made once for a
layout.  The debug build opened the document in @early_open_after@ s.
Memory: on 12 November the document used @early_mem_before@ MB after the
layout, 39 bytes for each byte of the file.  The main fix cut a token from 24
bytes to four, with the data of a marker in a table.  The document then used
@early_mem_after@ MB.
Cross references: after every change the Editor searched the table of links
and the table of sections from the start for each of the 6,000 cross
references of the Reference Manual (753 pages).  A typed character took 69 ms.
The table is now asked in the order of the document and sections are found by
a hash of their numbers.  A typed character takes 6.6 ms.
.h2 Survey [memory-survey]
.ix Counting allocator
On 18 November the project maintainer asked where each byte of a document is,
by function.  A counting allocator, a library loaded before the program,
replaces the allocation functions.  It counts live bytes for each place of
allocation, identified by the return addresses of four frames, and maps the
addresses to function names afterwards.  The Editor ran a script that opens a
document, goes to its end, searches and saves after one typed character, and
the script read the peak resident memory of each step from the
system.{fn:The peak resident memory is the highest size of the resident set of
a process that the system keeps.  It includes the program, the libraries and
the stack.}  A script made the documents from a text of 2.9 million words,
with @words_per_page@ words to a page, from 1 to @pages_big@ pages.
{Tab:shadow} breaks down the memory of @pages_mid@ pages after the cursor moved
to the end.  The first measurement had guessed a layout of the whole document
and a backup copy at the save.  Neither exists.  The memory found was a second
copy of the text, the Undo shadow.
.tabcap shadow Memory of a document of @pages_mid@ pages (MB)
.table 41 7 10
What | Before | After
.datarows shadow
.end
.h2 Reductions [memory-reduced]
The Undo shadow is made at the first key and compared with the document after
each key that changed it.  It was a clone of every block, at four bytes a
token: @shadow_text_before@ MB in {tab:shadow}.  Now the text of a column
without markers is packed, at one byte for a plain character.  The change saves
@shadow_saved_mid@ MB at
@pages_mid@ pages and @shadow_saved_big@ MB at @pages_big@, about two thirds of
the shadow.  The first key is faster too: the move to the end of a document of
@pages_big@ pages took @end_before_ms@ ms and takes @end_after_ms@.  The risk is
that the shadow decides what Undo restores.  A first change sized the
save buffer exactly by counting first: it saved up to half a file of memory
and caused the slower save of {x:memory-slower}.
.h2 Results [memory-results]
The measurement of 20 November used another machine, plain documents of
@words_per_page@ words to a page, the median of three runs of a fresh Editor
and a release build.  Before is the main line before the memory work.
{Tab:memory} gives the memory.
.tabcap memory Memory of the terminal Editor (MB, before / after)
.table 7 12 12 11 12
Pages | Open | End | Search | Save
.datarows memory
.end
{Fig:memorysave} shows the save column.  The memory at the open is the same
before and after, because the Undo copy is made at the first key.  After a
save at the largest size the memory is a third lower:
.center
{eq:\frac{{@mem_big_before@ - @mem_big_after@}}{{@mem_big_before@}}
 \approx @mem_ratio@}

The same holds at @pages_mid@ pages (@mid_before@ to @mid_after@ MB,
@mid_percent@
percent).  The X11 Editor needs @x11_more@ MB more than the terminal Editor at
every size.  Open and search take the same time before and after; the move to
the end takes @end_move_before@ ms before and @end_move_after@ ms after at
@pages_big@ pages.  Thirteen documents edited with the same keys in the old
and the new Editor were saved as the same bytes.
.pic pictures/story-memory-save.png 4 Memory after a save. [memorysave]
.h2 Slower save [memory-slower]
The first version of the memory work made a save slower on 18 November, and
the first survey did not see it.  The exact buffer needed a run over every
token that only counted bytes, @count_big_ms@ ms at @pages_big@ pages, before a
second run that wrote them.  A save of the largest document took
@save_slow_ms@ ms instead of @save_base_ms@, with pages of 735 words on the
first
machine.  The fix writes the bytes in one run into a buffer that starts at an
estimate and is cut to the file size at the end.  The memory after a save is
the same, and 17 saved documents of 17 have the same bytes.  {Tab:savetime}
gives the
later measurement: the build with the memory work alone
saves in @save_memory_only_ms@ ms at @pages_big@ pages.  The final build is
slower
than the build before the memory work (@save_final_ms@ against @save_before_ms@
ms),
because the main line has more changes by now.
.tabcap savetime Time of a save (ms) of three builds
.table 8 10 26 14
Pages | Before | Memory work only | Final
.datarows save
.end
.h2 Findings not acted on [memory-notdone]
- The token takes four bytes for every character, and the text is
  @text_mb_mid@ of the @total_mb_mid@ MB at @pages_mid@ pages.  A two-byte token
  would save @token2_saving_big@ MB at @pages_big@ pages and changes every
  function that reads a text, in about 60 files.
- A save writes through one buffer of the file size, @save_buffer_big@ MB at
  @pages_big@ pages.  The check that nothing is written when the bytes are the
  same needs the bytes first.
- The load reads the whole file before it makes the tokens.
